# voyage_toward — success
#
# Setting out across country for somewhere seen from a roof. The one verb that moves between areas
# without walking a path, and the payoff for the climb that reached the viewpoint.
#
# A landscape is a point of interest in the high area, not remembered knowledge, so it is observed
# like anything else and the observation offers the journey. That is the whole of the refactor: the
# verb it replaced needed a separate OBSERVE HORIZON to record revealed areas onto the PoV, and
# nothing else in the game gated on accumulated point-of-view state — so the reveal was written and
# the verb refresh, which builds its own PoV, could never see it.
#
# The roof is reached by climbing the building's wall; --start-area puts the script up there directly.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --auto-dialogue --location-type test --location-id 0 --start-area "Test Crown" --period dawn --observe-only "Distant Test Yard"

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60

strategy succeed
goal voyage_toward
advance 12 180
regions

click keyword 0
choose 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
regions

click action 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180
expect SUCCESS
# The OUTCOME, not just the banner. "SUCCESS" is the dice result — it is printed from
# result.Succeeded and says nothing about what the verb did, so a verb whose SuccessReports
# stopped producing anything would still pass every other assertion here. This is the chip
# the outcome block carries, and it is the only line that proves the verb had an effect.
expect Moved to:

# What makes this a voyage_toward test rather than an any-verb test.
expect-verb voyage_toward
# The outcome TYPE, asserted by its stable id rather than by chip wording. `expect <chip>`
# proves what the player was told; this proves what actually ran. See --outcome-audit.
expect-outcome area_move
quit
